# [DD-13b.W1.2] A `config` BLOCK'S `pcrec` LINE REACHING PAST COMPILE
# OPTIONS.
#
# `pcrec <raw>` is re-parsed by the CLI's OWN option parser (§1.5), so a
# flag cannot mean one thing on the command line and another here. That
# reuse is the point and it is also the hazard: the command line's parser
# also understands `-o`, `-p`, a pattern, the query modes and `--source`,
# none of which a config block may set — `-p` in particular would
# silently overrule the target's own declared prefix.
#
# The refusal is ONE test over the whole tail of the parser's state
# rather than a list of flag names, so a flag added to pcrec tomorrow is
# covered without an edit here. `-p` is the witness because it is the one
# that would otherwise be SILENT: an artifact under the wrong prefix
# compiles perfectly.
config sneaky
  pcrec -p notmyprefix

target t = a_run with sneaky

pattern a+
name a_run
m "aaa" 0 3
